## Data — CC BY-SA 4.0

Applies to everything in `dataset/`, `cases/`, `index/` and `docs/`.

Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International.
Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode

You may share and adapt this material for any purpose, including commercially,
provided you give appropriate credit, indicate if changes were made, and
distribute any adapted database under this same licence.

CC BY-SA 4.0 covers the sui generis database right as well as copyright, which
matters in jurisdictions that recognise it.

### What ShareAlike does and does not require

It bites on **adapted databases**. If you correct, extend, merge or restructure
this data and publish the result as a dataset, that dataset must carry CC BY-SA
4.0 too.

It does **not** bite on analysis. Fitting a model to this data, computing
statistics from it, or publishing a paper about it does not create an adapted
database. Cite the source and publish wherever you like, under whatever terms
your journal uses.

### Why ShareAlike rather than plain Attribution

Plain CC BY would allow this data to be absorbed into a closed or paid product.
The project exists because open, source-traceable criminological data is scarce;
a licence permitting its re-enclosure would work against the reason for building
it. The cost is accepted knowingly: ShareAlike cannot be combined with data
under incompatible licences, which will occasionally prevent a merge that
plain CC BY would have allowed.

The licence changed from CC BY 4.0 to CC BY-SA 4.0 in November 2026, while the
repository had a single contributor. Anything obtained under the earlier licence
remains available on its original terms; CC BY grants are not revocable.

## Use restrictions

The licences above grant broad rights. Independently of them, `ETHICS.md`
states that this dataset is not suitable for predicting individual
dangerousness, for screening or scoring individuals, for operational decisions
about any specific person, or for training generative models to produce
offender narratives.

The sample contains only offenders who were caught and convicted. Any model
fitted to it learns the characteristics of *detected* offending, which is a
statement about policing rather than about offenders. Inference from this data
to living individuals is invalid.

This is a statement about validity, not a licence condition. It is here because
someone will try it anyway.
